WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:00.450
Good morning John

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.060
in your last video you were talking about how sometimes huge shifts in global power structures result in short-term

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.490
catastrophe, and that made me think about how deeply

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:15.270
wonderful it is that we have gotten through the last 73 years as a nuclear species without completely destroying ourselves.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:21.200
There are a 195-ish countries; only nine of them have access to nuclear weapons.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.960
North Korea with a per-capita GDP of $1300

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.960
has proven that you don't have to have a ton of money to have a nuclear arsenal.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:37.060
So why, why, why don't more countries have nukes?! This seems frankly like a tremendous success.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:42.260
One that should be celebrated, and studied, and talked about, and fretted over maintaining.

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:47.699
So let's talk about why, more than 73 years after the development of the first nuclear weapons,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:53.920
so few countries have access to them. Caveat. I am NOT an expert in international relations,

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:59.879
though I did talk to some of those, and I recorded one of them as an interview that's on Hankschannel right now.

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:01.979
But I will not be going deep into any of this,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.600
this is something that lots of people study for their whole lives,

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.840
the Wikipedia page on Iran's nuclear program by itself is more than 20,000 words.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.040
You can, I think, say a lot with some broad strokes.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.259
Let's start with the list, in order. The US tested its first nuclear bomb in 1945,

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:23.669
the USSR followed in 1949, the UK and 1957, France in 1960, followed by China in 1964.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.640
That was a big spurt and it will become important later.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:31.409
Israel definitely has secret nukes, and may have had them as early as the 1960s

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:36.220
But they've never tested one or admitted to having them. India developed nuclear weapons in the 70s.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:41.580
South Africa built but probably never tested fission bombs in the 1980s before dismantling them.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:47.580
In 1991, three countries briefly had nuclear weapons because when they started existing they already had them:

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.300
Ukraine, Belarus and, Kazakhstan all had nuclear weapons when the USSR collapsed.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:57.780
in 1994 they gave all those weapons back to Russia in exchange for assurances that Russia would never challenge their borders,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.780
Which seems to have turned out just fine.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:04.859
Pakistan surprised the world with a nuclear test in 1998, and North Korea's first test was 2006.

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:09.000
If we look at it in volume the US and Russia, now Russia *not the USSR*,

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.220
have the *vast majority* of Nuclear weapons.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:18.080
Everyone else is pretty much tied for last, except for North Korea which is extra last.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.880
and this isn't to say that other countries haven't tried.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.660
There have been nuclear programs in Brazil, Argentina, Libya, Iran, Taiwan, South Korea,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.320
there are more states that have tried to get nuclear weapons then there are states that have them.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.000
The story of why is complicated.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.780
We're gonna start with the 1960s when there was that big spurt in new nuclear powers, ending with China.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.000
that was seen rightfully as scary.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.580
Possibly, especially, because it ended with China, a country that was um...

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:52.560
Europeans were like, well, that's not a European country. Those people aren't white and also Mao Zedong is a pretty scary dude.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.320
Countries were exploring nuclear programs just because their neighbours were,

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.580
and if they didn't know their neighbour wasn't, they were gonna do it.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.660
and the international community was realizing that this was gonna result in a fully nuclear world.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:07.549
But a lot of people in the 60s didn't see this as unusual or scary, it was like,

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.430
Okay well, there have been new weapons before, there's a new weapon again and

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:14.240
the major militaries are gonna have access to this weapon.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.380
But an arms race was going on, and people were realizing that

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:23.840
as destructive as the bombs dropped in Japan were, they were only a fraction of what could be done.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:28.420
The Cold War started raging, people were building bomb shelters, students were having drills in their schools

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:33.110
And I think the world was kind of realizing what it would be like to have a world war,

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.340
With nuclear powers on both sides.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:38.989
So some countries got together at the UN to create a treaty that said

00:03:38.989 --> 00:03:41.660
"Hey...uhhh...look...STOP."

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.540
Everyone signed this piece of paper is saying that if you don't already have nukes

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.479
you will not pursue nuclear weapons programs.

00:03:47.479 --> 00:03:50.480
And over the years since, every country has been like

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:52.830
actually

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:53.900
Yeah.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.720
except for India, Pakistan and, Israel,

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:59.240
who basically just texted the UN one of those shrugging emoticons (¯\_(ツ)_/¯)

00:03:59.246 --> 00:04:03.653
All of those countries, though It took varying amounts of time, subsequently developed nuclear weapons.

00:04:03.653 --> 00:04:06.349
The only country to have withdrawn from that treaty, North Korea.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:10.579
So if you've signed that treaty, it's literally against international law to develop nuclear weapons.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.738
But you don't have to sign the treaty,

00:04:12.738 --> 00:04:17.180
as South Sudan proved when it became a country in 2011 and did not sign the treaty.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:17.820
At least yet.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.400
So why do countries sign it?

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.920
and why does so few countries have nuclear weapons?

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:23.960
Let's do a list:

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.980
One: It's frickin' expensive to purify uranium-235, though it is getting cheaper,

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:35.080
Two: It is also expensive to have the whole international community not buy anything from your country

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.760
and not sell anything to your country

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.260
so that raises the price of violating that treaty.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.860
Three: It's generally good to be like a productive member of the international community

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.520
and to just agree with the things that everyone agrees with.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.580
Four: International laws against selling nuclear materials,

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.220
except in very specific circumstances,

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:54.280
have actually been really successful.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.540
Number five: The United States has traditionally had a lot of really good relationships with its allies,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:03.480
and has promised to protect them with its giant arsenal of nuclear warheads.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.640
Number six: the 73 years since World War II have been remarkably prosperous and peaceful

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:11.260
Seven: We got very very lucky

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:14.460
There is no guarantee that if we somehow ran this experiment again,

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.160
that we would end up being in such a good situation.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.680
and finally Number eight: for those and also many other reasons including humanitarian ones

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.320
Most countries don't want nuclear weapons.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.900
They just don't want them.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.980
International Relations experts talk about a norm against nuclear weapons.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.760
They're seen as taboo,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.120
as if developing a nuclear weapons program

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.100
Is kind of seen as like wading into the muck.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:42.100
And if you look around at the people who are creating these weapons programs in recent years,

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:44.000
It is. It's kind of a mucky place.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.630
I was surprised when I started researching this video that South Africa had a nuclear program.

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:48.960
Why did that happen?

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.929
Because they were being ostracised by the international community for having an explicitly racist government.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:58.609
Unfortunately,
the main tool we have to discourage countries from developing nuclear weapons

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:03.080
Can sometimes force them even deeper into that muck.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.569
A country, whether that's the leader or its people or both,

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.420
are only gonna want to develop nuclear weapons right now

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:11.760
if they are feeling threatened by the International order

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:14.500
and that happens when the country is some combination of

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:15.480
unstable,

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:16.200
autocratic,

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.049
or has bad relationships with its neighbors.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:19.940
I'm not saying that like we should do it a different way.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:20.840
I don't know.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.360
But sanctioning countries does hurt their economies, and further isolates them

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.580
from the benefits of being part of the international community.

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:32.100
And of course the people who are hurt most by sanctions aren't the leaders and government palaces,

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:34.560
unless there's some kind of revolution, it's the poor.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.260
And the more unstable and disconnected they are,

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:40.489
the easier it is to tell a story about how the whole world is out to get them and

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:41.660
they need to protect themselves.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:47.509
And that is how countries like North Korea and, it's feared, maybe someday Iran, slip through these cracks.

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:51.530
So then we end up in a world where all the new entrants to like the 'nuclear club'

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.958
Which is a weird one to be in, not that I'm not.

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:57.200
Are the least stable countries.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.160
Both because sanctions, somewhat, maybe destabilized them.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:06.380
But also because stable countries who are productive members of the international community don't want nukes.

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:07.400
Why would they?

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:07.900
They're expensive,

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:09.040
they're illegal,

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:10.180
they're hard to maintain,

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.660
and they don't provide clear benefit.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:18.420
And that one is so important, even though I put it on my pinky finger, which is little. Because that's only true,

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:20.490
unless,

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.639
There are clear benefits to having nuclear weapons.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.660
Like increased legitimacy,

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:26.140
and access

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:28.910
and power, unless the world becomes less stable and citizens of a country

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:30.980
feel that they need to protect themselves

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:35.900
unless long-standing alliances that have held the world together for a long long time start to erode

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:36.400
unless

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.340
international law becomes less powerful

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:44.540
unless the work of generations of experts is not respected and upheld and continued.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:47.640
The barriers to making nuclear weapons are falling,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:50.780
and the penalties for doing it are becoming less effective.

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:52.680
So countries that want nuclear weapons,

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.420
it's gonna be easier for them to get them and so the path forward,

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.360
Maybe, is to do what we've been doing, which is to create a world

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.800
Where there are less benefits to having nuclear weapons.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:05.380
and so the list of countries that want nuclear weapons is short,

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:07.480
not because they're afraid of being penalized,

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.340
but because there's just not a lot of advantage to having them.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:14.660
Building that world was a complicated process that involved a lot of compromise

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:18.940
and a lot of hard work by good people. Like lots of them.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.700
Lots of people working hard together.

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:21.710
Because like any relationship,

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.820
Global relationships are lots of work.

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:28.429
and while we often only see the failings, these decades of non-proliferation

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:33.920
And a world in which nuclear weapons have only been used two times during war,

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.230
which I recognize was done by my country [U.S.]

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:40.069
That's a remarkable testament to people working together.

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:42.530
That's not to say that we didn't screw up at all during those 73 years

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.940
and it's not to say that we didn't have some terrifying near-misses.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:49.080
That we have gotten this far is one part luck,

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:55.460
one part hard work by thoughtful people dedicated to peace and stability,

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.060
and one part even more luck.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:00.700
And I just want to say thank you to the people who have worked on that.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:01.900
However you worked on it.

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:04.730
That's remarkable and also, like, dang.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:06.360
Thanks.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:11.089
And I worry that since it's been a fairly good 73 years, we may be getting a little complacent,

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:14.749
We maybe are forgetting how hard we worked for this outcome,

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:19.730
how bad it could have gone, and how much we need to keep working

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.120
To keep this stuff together

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.340
because I'm not saying it couldn't have gone better, but we have done remarkably well.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.620
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:30.230
*Unless!* No. Kidding! I'm kidding.

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:32.620
Thank you to Katherine Gill and Jeffrey Lewis

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:33.800
for consulting with me on this video,

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.800
You can see my interview with Jeffrey over on Hankschannel.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.659
It was a good time. It's about half an hour long.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.920
We have lots of good chats.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:43.489
And if you're interested in learning more about something completely different

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.120
That is also in the news,

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.300
We did a series on the US Court system

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:51.000
over on youtube.com/complexly,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.140
which is Complexly's new Youtube channel just for our production company

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.260
where we can try out new things and have a bit of a playground.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.410
All right, bye bye.

